uaegfx only: keep gfxboard.c's hardware boards out of the build

sources/src/gfxboard.c is WinUAE's emulation of the real RTG cards - Picasso II and IV,
CyberVision, Piccolo, Voodoo, Permedia and the rest - and its whole body sits behind
#ifdef PICASSO96. Turning PICASSO96 on therefore tries to compile 7000 lines that this build
cannot take:

  * it is gfxboard.cpp renamed, and does not compile as C99 (it uses a struct tag as a type
    name, among other things);
  * the boards are driven through the PCem device layer in sources/src/pcem, which is not in
    Makefile.common at all.

So the guard becomes PICASSO96 && WITH_GFXBOARD_HARDWARE, and nothing defines the second. That
is not a loss of anything that worked before - none of it has ever been built in this port -
and it leaves PICASSO96 meaning exactly "uaegfx", which is the board Peekaboo wants: the one
that is a plain Zorro RAM window picasso96.c owns, with no chip to emulate.

The file already had an #else block of stubs for the PICASSO96-off case, and that is now what
gets compiled. It needed extending: the rest of the tree only calls gfxboard_vsync_handler(),
gfxboard_toggle(), gfxboard_rtg_disable() and a dozen others once PICASSO96 is on, so they had
never been missed. "No hardware board here" is the right answer to every one of them.

Two are not stubs. gfxboard_get_configname() and gfxboard_get_configtype() answer for the two
uaegfx types, which are not boards in this file's sense and never reached find_board() even
upstream: expansion.c reads the config type to decide whether the RTG window autoconfigs as
Zorro II or Zorro III, and cfgfile.c matches gfxcard_type= against the config name. Returning
0/NULL for them would silently make every board a Zorro II one, capped at 8 MB.
gfxboard_getrtgbuffer() and gfxboard_freertgbuffer() forward to uaegfx's own, so a host-side
screenshot of an RTG screen still works.

Applies to libretro-uae 2b0ed42fe565fb997a0627aaa8f44e0948b527f8 in the order given by
patches/series.txt.

